<!-- house:begin: rendered from config/house.php by composer update; this project's rules go after house:end and outrank it -->
# CLAUDE.md — routes

## Every route belongs to exactly one surface

Name the surface before adding a route. Surfaces have different trust levels, so a route in the wrong group is a
security change. A surface the project defines gets its own row in a table of the same shape after `house:end`:

| Group | Reachable by | Middleware |
|-------|--------------|------------|
<!-- unless:spa -->
| `guest` | anyone, unauthenticated | Fortify's sign-in, registration, password reset, two-factor and passkey-login routes: `guest`, throttled (below) |
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:spa -->
| `guest` | anyone, unauthenticated | Fortify's sign-in, registration, password reset, two-factor and passkey-login routes under `/api/auth`: `guest`, throttled (below) |
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:htmx -->
| `web` (authenticated) | signed-in users | `auth`, `verified` where required, CSRF; Blade + htmx; policies decide per-record access |
| `public` | the internet + crawlers | `Route::middleware('public')` (below) |
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:spa -->
| `api` | the SvelteKit app and every other client | `/api/v1`, `auth:sanctum`: the session cookie + XSRF token for the SvelteKit app (`statefulApi`), tokens for the rest; Resources only; policies decide per-record access |
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
| `api` (only if needed) | machines | `/api/v1`, `auth:sanctum` with tokens only (`sanctum.guard` is empty, so a session is 401 here), Resources only, own throttle |
<!-- endif -->
| `ops` | operators | `/horizon` behind the `viewHorizon` gate |
| `console` | scheduler + operator | not HTTP |
<!-- if:reverb -->
<!-- if:spa -->
| `channels` | signed-in users | `POST /api/broadcasting/auth`, `api` + `auth:sanctum` (`withBroadcasting`, `bootstrap/app.php`); callbacks in `routes/channels.php` |
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
| `channels` | signed-in users | callbacks in `routes/channels.php` |
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->

- **Guest throttling:** `throttle:auth-forms` is on every Fortify route (`config/fortify.php`) but limits only
  register, forgot-password and reset-password, which Fortify leaves unthrottled (the limiter is in
  `FortifyServiceProvider`). Fortify's own limiters (login, two-factor, passkeys; verification when on) still apply.
<!-- if:reverb -->
- **A channel's auth mirrors the policy** that gates the record, never a looser check.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:htmx -->
- **The `public` group**, declared explicitly in `routes/web.php`. `web.php` is loaded via `withRouting(then:)`, so
  **no route gets `web` implicitly**.
  - Middleware: `CachePublicResponse` (first; `app/Http/CLAUDE.md`), `throttle:public`
    (`config('{{app}}.public_per_minute')`, default 120) and `SubstituteBindings` (outside `web`, without it a
    missing slug renders a blank model instead of a 404).
  - `/fragment/…` routes add the `htmx` alias (`HtmxOnly`: 404 without `HX-Request`).
  - **No write routes**: no POST/PUT/DELETE, no forms. That absence is what makes "no auth, no CSRF, no moderation,
    no rate limiting, cache everything" correct. One input route breaks all five and records that in this file, after
    `house:end`, in the same commit.
  - **No `StartSession`**: a session cookie makes an anonymous page personal.
- **Prefetch**: the layout sets `hx-ext="preload"` on `<body>` (imported in `app.ts`); navigation blocks set
  `hx-boost`, card links add `preload="mouseover"` (`resources/CLAUDE.md`).
- **Named routes everywhere**: `route()` in Blade and in htmx attributes, never hand-typed paths.
- **Public URLs are SEO assets**: lowercase hyphenated slugs (binding on `slug`, never an id), no trailing-slash
  variants, one canonical URL per resource (301 the rest, and from a renamed route).
- **Listings**: `?page=n` is self-canonical with `rel=prev/next`. **Any filter, sort, view, or a page past the last,
  is `noindex,follow`, canonicalised to the unfiltered page.** Query keys are allow-listed per route; anything else is
  a 422.
- `sitemap.xml` is files a job writes after the data changes, never generated per request; `robots.txt` comes from a
  controller. Both skip the response cache and send `Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600`.
- **htmx fragment endpoints are ordinary routes in the page's group**: same auth, policy and throttle.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:htmx -->
- **Named routes everywhere**, `route()`; no hand-typed paths.
<!-- endif -->
- **Authenticated routes scope to `$request->user()`.** The user never comes from a path or body parameter.
- **A write route authorizes before it validates**: `->can('<ability>', '<model>')` on the route (the route-bound model,
  or the class for a create) runs before the FormRequest resolves (`app/Http/Controllers/CLAUDE.md`).
<!-- if:tenancy -->
- Every authenticated group touching tenant data adds `tenant` after its auth middleware
  (`app/Http/Middleware/CLAUDE.md`). No route has a tenant parameter, subdomain or prefix.
- **The tenant switcher**, `current-tenant.update` with the body field `tenant`:
<!-- if:spa -->
  `POST /api/v1/current-tenant`, answering the new current tenant; a token request answers 403, since a token's
  tenant is fixed.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
  `POST /current-tenant` in the authenticated `web` group, never in `api` (token-only, and a token's tenant is fixed);
<!-- if:htmx -->
  it answers `HX-Redirect` to the home route.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:htmx -->
  it answers the new current tenant as JSON.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->
  It sits under auth only, outside `tenant`, because a stale tab may switch. A tenant the user is not a member of is a
  404; otherwise it writes session `tenant_id` and the membership's `last_used_at`.
<!-- if:reverb -->
- Channels carrying tenant data are named `tenant.{tenantId}.…`; their callback checks membership of that tenant.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->
- Route-model binding does the lookup; `->scopeBindings()` on nested resources, so
  `/projects/{project}/tasks/{task}` cannot fetch another project's task.
- `routes/api.php` is loaded under `/api/v1` (`apiPrefix`, `bootstrap/app.php`). Every authenticated route sits in
  one group with `auth:sanctum` and `cache.headers:private;no_store`, and no closure returns a model. A public `GET`
  sits outside it and sets its own `cache.headers:public;max_age=…` when it may be cached.
<!-- if:spa -->
- The session probe: `GET /api/v1/auth/me` → `UserResource` (with `has_password` and `passkeys_count`), 401 when
  signed out. The SvelteKit app reads the user only from it.
<!-- if:tenancy -->
  It sits outside `tenant` and adds `current_tenant` (`{id, name, role}` or null) and `tenants`.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->

## Fortify

Fortify registers its own routes from `config/fortify.php`; never redeclare them.
<!-- if:spa -->
It is mounted at `/api/auth` (`prefix`), never under `/api/v1`: its routes are the package's and carry no version.
**`views` is off**, which takes three things, all in place:
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:htmx -->
<!-- unless:auth-pages -->
**`views` is off until the auth Blade pages exist** (`resources/CLAUDE.md`, Auth pages, owns the flip). That takes
three things, all in place:
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:auth-pages -->
**`views` is on**: Fortify's GET routes serve the Blade auth pages (`resources/CLAUDE.md`, Auth pages) through
`Fortify::viewPrefix('auth.')`. Its routes answer with pages and redirects, and with JSON only to a request that sends
`Accept: application/json`; `password.reset` is Fortify's own reset page.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:htmx -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
**`views` is off**, since the client renders every auth page. That takes three things, all in place:
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:auth-pages -->
- The GET pages (`login`, `register`, `password.request`, `password.reset`, `two-factor.login`, `password.confirm`)
  are not registered.
<!-- if:spa -->
  `GET /api/auth/login` answers 405 beside its POST twin, and `/login` is the SvelteKit app's.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
  `GET /login` and `/register` answer 405 beside their POST twins.
<!-- endif -->
- Every Fortify route runs `AcceptJson` (`fortify.middleware`, ahead of `auth` in the priority list). So Fortify and
  `auth` answer JSON (a guest 401, a 2FA sign-in `{"two_factor": true}`, an unconfirmed password 423) instead of
  redirecting to a page that does not exist.
<!-- if:spa -->
- `ResetPassword::createUrlUsing` and `VerifyEmail::createUrlUsing` (`FortifyServiceProvider`) point the mailed links
  at the SvelteKit pages `/reset-password/{token}?email=` and `/email/verify/{id}/{hash}?expires=&signature=`. The
  verify page calls `/api/auth/email/verify/…` with the same query from the browser.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
<!-- if:htmx -->
- `ResetPassword::createUrlUsing` (`FortifyServiceProvider`) builds the mailed link on Fortify's
  `/reset-password/{token}?email=` path: the reset page, once it is built.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:htmx -->
- `ResetPassword::createUrlUsing` (`FortifyServiceProvider`) builds the mailed link on Fortify's
  `/reset-password/{token}?email=` path: the client's reset page.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->

## Social sign-in (when the project builds it)

The policy is root `CLAUDE.md`, Auth. Every route goes in `routes/web.php`, in the `web` group with `throttle:social`
(the limiter comes with the build, `app/Providers/CLAUDE.md`). Never in `routes/api.php`: an identity provider's
redirect carries no stateful Referer or Origin.

<!-- if:spa -->
- `GET /api/auth/{provider}/redirect` (`social.redirect`) and `GET /api/auth/{provider}/callback`
  (`social.callback`). Failures land on `/login?social_error=<code>`; a 2FA user on `/two-factor-challenge`.
- `POST /api/auth/{provider}/link` (`social.link`): `AcceptJson`, `auth:sanctum`, `password.confirm`. It answers
  `{redirect: <provider URL>}`, and 423 until confirmed: `RequirePassword` decides with `expectsJson()`, and with
  views off there is no confirm page to redirect to.
- `DELETE /api/auth/{provider}` (`social.unlink`): `AcceptJson`, `auth:sanctum`, `password.confirm`.
- `GET /api/auth/providers`: the enabled providers as JSON, safe for a guest. The sign-in page renders its buttons
  from it.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
- `GET /auth/{provider}/redirect` (`social.redirect`) and `GET /auth/{provider}/callback` (`social.callback`).
<!-- if:htmx -->
- `POST /auth/{provider}/link` (`social.link`): `auth`, `password.confirm`. Never a GET, so no other site can start a
  link. Plain `auth` (the session), never `auth:sanctum`: Sanctum takes tokens only here.
- `DELETE /auth/{provider}` (`social.unlink`): `auth`, `password.confirm`.
- Both are plain forms (`@csrf`, `hx-boost="false"`; unlink adds `@method('DELETE')`), never htmx requests: link
  redirects to the provider, and an unconfirmed request gets a 302 to the confirm page.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:htmx -->
- `POST /auth/{provider}/link` (`social.link`): `AcceptJson`, `auth`, `password.confirm`. It answers
  `{redirect: <provider URL>}`, and 423 until confirmed: `RequirePassword` decides with `expectsJson()`, and with
  views off there is no confirm page to redirect to.
- `DELETE /auth/{provider}` (`social.unlink`): `AcceptJson`, `auth`, `password.confirm`.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:spa -->
- `GET /api/auth/social/confirm` (`social.confirm`): `signed`, the mailed link of a sign-up the provider did not vouch
  for (root `CLAUDE.md`, Auth). It completes only in the session that started the sign-up, then redirects into the
  SvelteKit app.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- unless:spa -->
- `GET /auth/social/confirm` (`social.confirm`): `signed`, the mailed link of a sign-up the provider did not vouch for
  (root `CLAUDE.md`, Auth). It completes only in the session that started the sign-up.
<!-- endif -->
- `{provider}` is checked per request against `services.socialite.providers` (unknown or off is a 404), never baked
  into the route pattern.

## Scheduler (`routes/console.php`)

- The scheduler is its own supervised process in the app container (Hosting). Without it, nothing runs and nothing
  says so.
- `->withoutOverlapping()` on every scheduled job; `->onOneServer()` once more than one container runs the scheduler.
- Stagger anything that fans out against a rate-limited external system.
- `routes/console.php` is the list of what runs (`schedule:list`); this file holds only the rules.

## Access logging

Log denials on gated surfaces with the request id: a client *trying* to reach what it may not is worth knowing.
<!-- house:end -->
